# Parity cases for ooxargs.
# The tool replaces xargs and must behave the same way.
# upstream: xargs
# tool: dist/ooxargs
#
# A match case must produce the same stdout, the same stderr, and the same
# exit code as the upstream tool. A differ case is a declared deviation: the
# exit code must still be the same, but the output may differ.
#
# The upstream tool is the reference. A tool that is not a drop-in
# replacement must fix the difference or declare the deviation here.
#
# The workflow installs the upstream tool. The parity runner reads this table
# from the repository root and runs every case with the same arguments
# against both tools. A case that the upstream tool does not understand is
# skipped, because the upstream cannot be the reference there.
#
# The cases come from the test recipe of this repository, so they are the
# arguments this tool already claims to accept.
#
# A case may be preceded by an input line. That line names the command that
# makes the input of the case and its arguments. The runner pipes that command
# into both tools, so a tool that works on a stream is compared on a stream.
#
# A case may be preceded by a setup line. That line is a command that
# prepares the input of the cases that follow it, such as the command that
# makes the file the tool reads. The runner runs it again before each of the
# two tools, so both see the same input even when one of them changes it.

setup echo "==> Running negative-trust verification suite..."
